  "name": "Groundwork",
  "url": "https://myground.work",
  "description": "Groundwork gives a clear picture of your most important work period. Everyone checks in privately. It shows where people agree, where they understand things differently, and where a conversation is needed.",
  "applicationCategory": "BusinessApplication",
  "operatingSystem": "Web",
  "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" }
})}</script>
<style>
*,*::before,*::after{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0;padding:0}

/* ── TOKENS ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
#s-land{
  --c-navy:#0C447C;
  --c-navy-d:#0A1628;
  --c-teal:#5DCAA5;
  --c-amber:#E8A94A;
  --c-text:#1A1916;
  --c-sub:#6B6560;
  --c-muted:#9B9590;
  --c-border:#E2E0DB;
  --c-bg:#EDECEA;
  --c-off:#F5F3EF;
  --c-white:#FFFFFF;
  --font:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',sans-serif;
}

body{font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',sans-serif;font-size:13px;color:#1A1916;min-height:100vh;background:#EDECEA;margin:0}

/* ── ANIMATIONS ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
@keyframes l-blink  {0%,100%{opacity:1}50%{opacity:0}}
@keyframes l-pulse  {0%,100%{box-shadow:0 0 0 0 rgba(93,202,165,.5)}50%{box-shadow:0 0 0 5px rgba(93,202,165,0)}}
@keyframes l-fadein {from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(8px)}to{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0)}}
@keyframes l-up     {from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(20px)}to{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0)}}

/* ── NAV ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-nav{
  position:sticky;top:0;z-index:100;
  background:rgba(237,236,234,.96);backdrop-filter:blur(8px);
  border-bottom:1px solid var(--c-border);
  display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;
  padding:0 20px;height:56px;
}
.l-nav-logo{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:9px;cursor:pointer;flex-shrink:0}
.l-nav-logo span{font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-navy);letter-spacing:-.02em}
.l-nav-links{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:2px}
.l-nav-link{
  font-size:13px;font-weight:500;color:var(--c-sub);background:none;border:none;
  cursor:pointer;padding:6px 11px;border-radius:6px;font-family:var(--font);
  transition:all .15s;white-space:nowrap;
}
.l-nav-link:hover{background:#E8E6E3;color:var(--c-text)}
.l-nav-link.active{color:var(--c-navy);font-weight:600}
.l-nav-ctas{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;flex-shrink:0}
.l-btn-ghost{
  font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--c-navy);background:none;
  border:1px solid #B5D4F4;cursor:pointer;padding:7px 14px;border-radius:6px;
  font-family:var(--font);white-space:nowrap;text-decoration:none;display:inline-block;
}
.l-btn-ghost:hover{background:#EEF4FB}
.l-btn-solid{
  font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-white);background:var(--c-navy);
  border:none;cursor:pointer;padding:7px 14px;border-radius:6px;
  font-family:var(--font);white-space:nowrap;transition:background .15s;
  text-decoration:none;display:inline-block;
}
.l-btn-solid:hover{background:#1557A0}

/* ── HERO ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-hero{
  position:relative;overflow:hidden;background:var(--c-navy-d);
  display:flex;align-items:center;
  /* IT WAS A FULL-VIEWPORT HERO WITH HALF A VIEWPORT OF CONTENT IN IT.
     min-height:calc(100svh - 56px) made it 844px tall on a 900px screen, holding
     about 420px of anything, so a third of the first screen was empty navy and
     the composition sat marooned in the top half of it.

     A full-height hero is a fine choice when the content fills it. This one does
     not, and it is better for it: ending a little above the fold shows the top of
     the next section, which is the report - the most convincing thing on the
     site - and gives a reason to scroll rather than a wall to get past. */
  min-height:520px;
  padding:44px 20px;
}
.l-startwell{
  display:flex;gap:12px;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap;
  max-width:820px;margin:0 auto 28px;
}
.l-sw-lead{
  font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;
  color:rgba(147,197,253,.75);margin:0 auto 14px;
}
.l-sw-step{
  flex:1;min-width:210px;display:flex;gap:11px;text-align:left;
  background:rgba(255,255,255,.05);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.1);
  border-radius:12px;padding:16px 18px;
}
.l-sw-n{
  flex-shrink:0;width:22px;height:22px;border-radius:50%;
  background:rgba(147,197,253,.16);color:#93C5FD;
  font-size:12px;font-weight:700;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
  margin-top:1px;
}
.l-sw-step p{font-size:13.5px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.78);line-height:1.6;margin:0}

/* THE HERO IS A COMPOSITION NOW, NOT A CENTRED PILE.
   It was nine elements and eight of them were centred text: eyebrow, headline,
   subhead, two problem lines, a reassurance, a label, three cards, two buttons.
   Four consecutive prose blocks in three greys with no rank between them. That
   cannot be fixed by adjusting the gaps, because there was no composition to
   adjust - a centred column has only one axis.

   Left column carries the argument, right column carries what you do. The
   vertical pile halves, the steps stop reading as a footnote under a wall of
   text, and nothing is cut. Below 900px it collapses back to the single centred
   stack, which is the right shape on a phone. */
.l-hero-two{
  display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.05fr .95fr;gap:52px;align-items:start;
  text-align:left;max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;
}
/* THE TWO COLUMNS HAVE TO SHARE AN EDGE, or they are two things near each other
   rather than one composition. They were vertically centred, so a 419px left
   column and a 283px right column met at no line at all and the steps floated in
   the middle of nothing. They start together now, and the steps are dropped by
   the height of the eyebrow so they line up with the top of the headline rather
   than with the label above it. */
.l-hero-two > div:last-child{padding-top:34px}
@media (max-width: 900px){ .l-hero-two > div:last-child{padding-top:0} }

/* The problem lines are a different BEAT from the promise above them, and they
   were reading as more of the same grey. A hairline gives them an edge without
   adding a box to a screen that already has enough of them. */
.l-hero-problem-block{border-left:2px solid rgba(147,197,253,.22);padding-left:16px;margin:0 0 26px}
@media (max-width: 900px){
  .l-hero-problem-block{border-left:none;padding-left:0;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto}
}
.l-hero-two .l-hero-label{margin-bottom:14px}
.l-hero-two .l-startwell{flex-direction:column;margin:0;max-width:none}
.l-hero-two .l-sw-lead{margin:0 0 14px}
.l-hero-two .l-hero-btns{justify-content:flex-start}
@media (max-width: 900px){
  /* Left, not centred. The sections below the hero have always been left-aligned
     on a phone, so a centred hero meant the page changed its mind about where
     text starts within the first scroll. */
  .l-hero-two{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:30px;text-align:left;max-width:620px}
  .l-hero-two .l-hero-btns{justify-content:flex-start}
}
.l-fx-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:14px;margin-top:8px}
.l-fx{background:#fff;border:1px solid #E2E0DB;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 22px}
.l-fx-lead{grid-column:1 / -1;background:var(--c-navy-d);border-color:var(--c-navy-d)}
/* Kept for the shape it names: a follower that has to close an odd row. There
   are four followers now, so nothing uses it. */
.l-fx-last{grid-column:1 / -1}
/* The lead's account beside everyone else's. Two columns is the whole point -
   they are meant to be read against each other - so on a phone, where they
   cannot be, they stack. */
/* THE FOUR-UP THE PRODUCT ALREADY COMPUTES. quickRead in board.service.ts:
   Aligned? / Moving? / Stuck? / Solid?, each with its own sub-line and tone. */
.l-qr{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:1px;background:#E8E6E3}
.l-qr div{background:#fff;padding:11px 14px}
.l-qr .k{font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px}
.l-qr .v{font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.25;margin-bottom:2px}
.l-qr .s{font-size:11px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.4}
@media (max-width: 620px){ .l-qr{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr} }

/* What each person moved. A row of work, not a row of opinion. */
.l-del{display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:flex-start;padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #F0EEE9}
.l-del:last-child{border-bottom:none}
.l-del .who{flex:0 0 108px;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.45}
.l-del .did{flex:1;font-size:13.5px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.55}
.l-del .ev{flex:0 0 auto;font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;color:#085041;background:#E8F8F5;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 9px;white-space:nowrap}
.l-del .ev-none{color:#8A5C1A;background:#FBF1E0}
@media (max-width: 620px){
  .l-del{flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px}
  .l-del .who{flex:0 0 100%}
  .l-del .did{flex:0 0 100%}
}
/* SCENARIOS SIDE BY SIDE, SCROLLED THROUGH.
   One sample report answers "what does this look like" for the person whose
   situation happens to match it, and leaves everybody else guessing. Stacking a
   second doubled an already long page. So they sit in a row you scroll or swipe,
   with tabs for anyone who would rather jump.

   Scroll-snap and overflow only - no carousel library, no autoplay, and it works
   with a trackpad, a touchscreen, the tab key and a scrollbar. */
/* align-items MATTERS HERE, and the default was wrong in a way that reads as a
   broken page rather than a styling nit. A flex row stretches every child to the
   tallest, so all four cases became 3213px - the height of the longest one - and
   the shorter ones carried two thousand pixels of empty white underneath. You
   scroll, and scroll, and nothing is there. That is what "I cannot get to the
   other pages" actually was. */
.l-cases{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:24px;overflow-x:auto;scroll-snap-type:x mandatory;scroll-behavior:smooth;padding:2px 2px 10px;margin:0 -2px;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch}
.l-case{flex:0 0 100%;min-width:0;scroll-snap-align:start}
.l-cases::-webkit-scrollbar{height:8px}
.l-cases::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb{background:#D8D5CF;border-radius:8px}
.l-case-tabs{display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px}
.l-case-tab{font-family:inherit;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:600;padding:7px 14px;border-radius:20px;border:1px solid var(--gw-border,#E2E0DB);background:#fff;color:#6B6560;cursor:pointer}
.l-case-tab[aria-selected="true"]{background:#0A1628;border-color:#0A1628;color:#fff}
.l-told{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:10px}
@media (max-width: 620px){ .l-told{grid-template-columns:1fr} }
.l-fx h3{font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-text);margin:0 0 6px}
.l-fx p{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--c-sub);line-height:1.65;margin:0}
.l-fx-lead h3{color:#fff;font-size:18px}
.l-fx-lead p{color:rgba(255,255,255,.8);font-size:14.5px}
.l-hero-inner{
  max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;width:100%;
  display:block;
}
.l-hero-label{
  font-size:10px;font-weight:600;color:rgba(147,197,253,.65);
  text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.14em;margin-bottom:20px;
}
.l-hero-h1{
  font-size:44px;font-weight:800;line-height:1.08;letter-spacing:-.04em;
  color:var(--c-white);margin:0 0 22px;min-height:160px;
}
.l-hero-sub{
  font-size:15px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.52);line-height:1.75;
  margin:0 0 10px;max-width:440px;
}
.l-hero-sub2{
  font-size:12px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.26);line-height:1.6;
  margin:0 0 40px;max-width:420px;
}
.l-hero-btns{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.l-scn-chip{background:rgba(255,255,255,0.08);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.88);font-family:var(--font);font-size:13px;font-weight:600;padding:8px 14px;border-radius:20px;cursor:pointer;transition:background .15s;text-decoration:none}
.l-scn-chip:hover{background:rgba(255,255,255,0.18)}
.l-hero-btn-p{
  padding:13px 24px;border-radius:6px;background:var(--c-navy);color:var(--c-white);
  font-size:14px;font-weight:700;border:none;cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--font);
  transition:background .15s;text-decoration:none;display:inline-block;
}
.l-hero-btn-p:hover{background:#1557A0}
.l-hero-btn-g{
  padding:13px 24px;border-radius:6px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.07);
  color:rgba(255,255,255,.8);font-size:14px;font-weight:600;
  border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.12);cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--font);
  transition:background .15s;text-decoration:none;display:inline-block;
}
.l-hero-btn-g:hover{background:rgba(255,255,255,.12)}

.l-learn-more{
  font-size:12px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.4);margin:18px 0 0;cursor:pointer;
}
.l-learn-more:hover{color:rgba(255,255,255,.65)}

/* Primary CTA attention cue: slow breathing glow + periodic sheen sweep */
@keyframes l-cta-breathe{
  0%,100%{ box-shadow:0 4px 14px rgba(12,68,124,.35), 0 0 0 0 rgba(147,197,253,0); }
  50%{ box-shadow:0 6px 22px rgba(12,68,124,.55), 0 0 0 6px rgba(147,197,253,.12); }
}
@keyframes l-cta-sheen{
  0%,16%{ left:-70%; } 32%{ left:130%; } 100%{ left:130%; }
}
.l-cta-pulse{ position:relative; overflow:hidden; animation:l-cta-breathe 2.8s ease-in-out infinite; }
.l-cta-pulse::after{
  content:''; position:absolute; top:0; left:-70%; width:45%; height:100%;
  background:linear-gradient(120deg, transparent, rgba(255,255,255,.4), transparent);
  transform:skewX(-20deg); pointer-events:none;
  animation:l-cta-sheen 5s ease-in-out infinite;
}
.l-cta-pulse:hover{ transform:translateY(-1px); animation-play-state:paused; }
@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
  .l-cta-pulse{ animation:none; }
  .l-cta-pulse::after{ display:none; }
}

/* ── FEED PANEL ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-feed-panel{
  background:rgba(255,255,255,.04);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.09);
  border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;
}
.l-feed-hdr{
  display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;
  padding:11px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.07);
  background:rgba(255,255,255,.03);
}
.l-mobile-checkin{display:none}
.l-feed-dot{
  width:7px;height:7px;border-radius:50%;background:#5DCAA5;
  display:inline-block;animation:l-pulse 2s ease-in-out infinite;flex-shrink:0;
}
.l-feed-body{min-height:230px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:flex-end}
.l-feed-foot{
  display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;
  padding:10px 14px;border-top:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.06);gap:4px;
}
.l-stat-val{font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-white);text-align:center;transition:color .4s}
.l-stat-lbl{font-size:9px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.28);text-align:center;margin-top:2px;letter-spacing:.02em}
.l-feed-item{
  animation:l-fadein .4s ease forwards;
  padding:8px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.04);
}
.l-feed-item:last-child{border-bottom:none}

/* ── NET BG ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
/* The decorative net has no viewBox, so its children are literal pixel
   coordinates - the longest line ends at x=800. Below about 800px wide that
   line sticks out past the viewport and the whole page scrolls sideways
   (measured: scrollWidth 697 against clientWidth 637). Clipping it keeps the
   decoration and stops it dragging the page. GW-008. */
.l-net{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;pointer-events:none;opacity:.07;overflow:hidden}

/* ── SECTIONS ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-sec{width:100%}
.l-wrap{max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 20px}
/* ONE COLUMN ORIGIN FOR THE WHOLE PAGE.
   Everything on this page was already left-aligned. The problem was that it was
   left-aligned to two different left edges: the hero ran in a 1000px container
   and every section below it in a 720px one, both centred. Measured at a 1100px
   window, the headline started at x=70 and every section heading below it at
   x=210 - so the moment you scrolled past the hero the whole page jogged 140px
   to the right, on every section, all the way down.

   That is the weirdness. It is not something you can name while looking at it,
   which is why it survived this long, and it is why nothing on the page felt
   settled however the individual sections were arranged.

   One container now. Running text stays capped at 600px by .l-prose, because a
   1000px measure is unreadable; headings, panels and grids get the full width,
   which the report panel in particular wanted. */
.l-wrap-sm{max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 20px}

/* ONE MEASURE FOR EVERY PARAGRAPH OF ARGUMENT.
   The prose ran the full 720px container at 15px, which is around 100
   characters a line. Past about 75 the eye loses its place coming back for the
   next line, which was most of why the argument sections felt heavy. Headings
   keep the full width; only running text is capped. Not a word changes. */
.l-prose{max-width:600px}

/* SECTION 3's OBJECT: three beats.
   The three lines are the memorable part of "why leaders miss problems" and
   they were the least visible thing in it, set as bold paragraphs inside a
   column of grey ones. */
.l-beats{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px;margin:28px 0}
.l-beat{background:#fff;border:1px solid #E2E0DB;border-left:3px solid #C9C5BE;border-radius:10px;padding:16px 18px;font-size:15.5px;font-weight:600;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.5}

/* SECTION 5's OBJECT: two columns facing each other.
   "What your team gets" and "What Groundwork is not" are a contrast, and
   stacked vertically the reader never sees them together. */
.l-facing{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:14px;align-items:start}

/* SECTION 6's OBJECT: a checklist.
   Four things waiting for you when you get back. It is the most concrete
   promise on the page and it was set as a bulleted paragraph. Deliberately
   lighter than the report panel, which is the only document on this page. */
.l-back{background:#fff;border:1px solid #E2E0DB;border-radius:10px;padding:6px 20px;margin:0 0 18px}
.l-back-row{display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:flex-start;padding:13px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #F0EEE9;font-size:15px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.55}
.l-back-row:last-child{border-bottom:none}
.l-back-tick{flex-shrink:0;width:18px;height:18px;border-radius:50%;background:#E8F8F5;color:#085041;font-size:11px;font-weight:800;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;margin-top:1px}
.l-sec-label{
  font-size:10px;font-weight:600;color:var(--c-navy);
  text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.14em;margin-bottom:18px;opacity:.65;
}
.l-sec-h2{font-size:36px;font-weight:800;color:var(--c-text);letter-spacing:-.03em;line-height:1.15;margin-bottom:14px}
.l-sec-p{font-size:15px;color:var(--c-sub);line-height:1.75;margin-bottom:40px;max-width:500px}

/* ── PROBLEM ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-prob-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:14px;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto}
.l-prob-card{
  background:var(--c-white);border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--c-border);
  padding:16px 14px 16px 18px;position:relative;overflow:hidden;
}
.l-prob-card::before{
  content:'';position:absolute;left:0;top:0;bottom:0;width:3px;background:var(--c-navy);
}
.l-prob-title{font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-text);margin-bottom:4px}
.l-prob-desc{font-size:12px;color:var(--c-sub);line-height:1.55}

/* ── MOMENTS ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-mom-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:18px}
.l-mom-card{
  background:var(--c-white);border-radius:10px;border:1px solid var(--c-border);
  padding:26px 22px;border-top-width:3px;
}
.l-mom-card.start{border-top-color:var(--c-navy)}
.l-mom-card.recog{border-top-color:var(--c-amber)}
.l-mom-card.align{border-top-color:var(--c-teal)}
.l-mom-badge{
  display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;margin-bottom:14px;
}
.l-mom-badge-dot{width:7px;height:7px;border-radius:50%;flex-shrink:0;animation:l-pulse 2.5s ease-in-out infinite}
.l-mom-badge span{font-size:10px;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em}
.l-mom-h3{font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-text);margin-bottom:8px;letter-spacing:-.02em;line-height:1.3}
.l-mom-desc{font-size:13px;color:var(--c-sub);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:14px}
.l-mom-item{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;font-size:12px;color:var(--c-sub);line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:5px}
.l-dot{width:5px;height:5px;border-radius:50%;flex-shrink:0;margin-top:5px}
.l-dot-navy{background:var(--c-navy)}
.l-dot-amber{background:var(--c-amber)}
.l-dot-teal{background:var(--c-teal)}

/* ── DEMO ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-demo-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(250px,1fr));gap:10px;max-width:900px}
.l-demo-btn{
  display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:11px;padding:13px;border-radius:8px;
  background:var(--c-white);border:1px solid var(--c-border);cursor:pointer;
  font-family:var(--font);text-align:left;transition:all .15s;text-decoration:none;
}
.l-demo-btn:hover{border-color:#B5D4F4;background:#EEF4FB;transform:translateY(-1px)}
.l-demo-dot{width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:50%;flex-shrink:0;margin-top:3px}
.l-demo-title{font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-text);margin-bottom:2px}
.l-demo-desc{font-size:12px;color:var(--c-muted);line-height:1.4}

/* ── TRUST ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-trust-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:56px;align-items:center}
.l-trust-item{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:13px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #E8E6E3}
.l-trust-item:last-child{border-bottom:none}
.l-trust-dot{width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--c-navy);flex-shrink:0;margin-top:5px}
.l-trust-strong{font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-text);display:block;margin-bottom:2px}
.l-trust-p{font-size:12px;color:var(--c-sub);line-height:1.55;margin:0}

/* ── PRICING ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-price-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:18px;margin-bottom:32px}
.l-price-card{border-radius:10px;padding:26px}
.l-price-card.free{background:var(--c-off);border:1px solid var(--c-border)}
.l-price-card.paid{background:var(--c-navy)}
.l-price-tier{font-size:11px;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em;margin-bottom:14px}
.l-price-amt{font-size:34px;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:4px}
.l-price-sub{font-size:13px;margin-bottom:22px}
.l-price-item{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;font-size:13px;margin-bottom:5px}

/* ── STEPS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-step{display:flex;gap:18px;padding:26px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #E8E6E3;align-items:flex-start}
.l-step:last-child{border-bottom:none}
.l-step-num{
  width:30px;height:30px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--c-navy);
  color:var(--c-white);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
  font-size:12px;font-weight:700;flex-shrink:0;margin-top:1px;
}
.l-step-title{font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-text);margin-bottom:5px;letter-spacing:-.01em}
.l-step-desc{font-size:13px;color:var(--c-sub);line-height:1.65}

/* ── CTA ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-cta-sec{background:var(--c-navy-d);text-align:center;padding:80px 20px}
.l-cta-h2{font-size:40px;font-weight:800;color:var(--c-white);letter-spacing:-.04em;line-height:1.1;margin-bottom:14px}
.l-cta-p{font-size:16px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.4);margin-bottom:32px;font-style:italic}

/* ── FOOTER ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-footer{background:#091525;padding:36px 20px}
.l-footer-inner{max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:16px}

/* ── SCROLL FADE ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
.l-fade{opacity:0;transform:translateY(18px);transition:opacity .5s ease,transform .5s ease}
.l-fade.in{opacity:1;transform:translateY(0)}

/* ══ TABLET (≤900px) ═══════════════════════════════════════════ */
@media(max-width:900px){
  .l-hero-inner{padding:44px 20px 40px}
  .l-hero-h1{font-size:36px;min-height:auto}
  .l-prob-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:12px}
  .l-mom-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .l-trust-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:32px}
  .l-price-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .l-wrap{padding:52px 20px}
  .l-wrap-sm{padding:52px 20px}
  .l-cta-h2{font-size:32px}
  .l-footer-inner{justify-content:center;text-align:center}
}

/* ══ NARROW LAPTOP / TABLET (≤820px) ═══════════════════════════ */
/* The nav is logo + page links + two CTAs on one row. Below about 820px that
   row is wider than the viewport, so the page scrolled sideways and the
   "Get started" button was clipped off the right edge - the first thing a
   visitor on a small laptop or a tablet saw. The mobile rule already solves
   this by dropping the page links, but it only applied at 620px, leaving a
   200px band where the nav overflowed. Same fix, applied where it starts to
   be needed. GW-008. */
@media(max-width:820px){
  .l-nav-links{display:none}
}

/* ══ MOBILE (≤620px) ═══════════════════════════════════════════ */
@media(max-width:620px){
  /* Nav - keep both buttons, hide page links */
  .l-nav{padding:0 14px;height:52px}
  .l-nav-links{display:none}
  .l-btn-ghost{font-size:12px;padding:6px 10px}
  .l-btn-solid{font-size:12px;padding:6px 10px}

  /* Hero - mobile */
  .l-hero{padding:0}
  .l-hero-inner{padding:28px 16px 24px}
  .l-hero-label{font-size:9px;margin-bottom:10px}
  .l-hero-h1{font-size:20px !important;min-height:auto;margin-bottom:10px !important}
  .l-hero-sub{font-size:13px;margin-bottom:6px;max-width:100%}
  .l-hero-sub2{display:none}
  /* hero subtext smaller on mobile */
  .l-hero-inner p{font-size:14px !important;margin-bottom:16px !important}

  /* Start-well steps stack to a single column on mobile */
  .l-startwell{flex-direction:column;gap:10px;margin-bottom:20px}
  .l-sw-step{min-width:0;width:100%;padding:14px 16px}
  .l-sw-lead{font-size:10px;margin-bottom:10px}

  /* Functionality grid stacks on mobile */
  .l-fx-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:10px}
  .l-beats{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:8px;margin:20px 0}
  .l-beat{padding:13px 15px;font-size:15px}
  .l-facing{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:10px}
  .l-prose{max-width:none}
  .l-fx{padding:16px 18px}
  .l-fx-lead h3{font-size:16px}

  /* Primary CTAs - primary full width, sign in text link */
  .l-hero-btns{flex-direction:column;gap:8px;margin-bottom:0}
  .l-hero-btn-p{
    width:100%;text-align:center;padding:13px 14px;
    font-size:14px;border-radius:8px;
  }
  .l-hero-btn-g{
    width:100%;text-align:center;padding:8px 14px;
    font-size:13px;border-radius:8px;
    background:transparent !important;border-color:rgba(255,255,255,0.15) !important;
  }

  /* Mobile check-in CTA - additional prominent button */
  .l-mobile-checkin{
    display:block !important;
    width:100%;padding:16px 20px;margin-bottom:24px;
    border-radius:8px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.1);
    color:white;font-size:15px;font-weight:600;
    border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.2);
    cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--font);
    text-align:center;
  }

  /* Feed panel on mobile - compact, card-like */
  .l-feed-panel{
    border-radius:10px 10px 0 0;
    border-left:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none;
    margin:0 -16px;
  }
  .l-feed-body{min-height:180px}

  /* Sections */
  .l-wrap{padding:32px 16px}
  .l-wrap-sm{padding:32px 16px}
  /* Description section: left-align, smaller text on mobile */
  .l-what-desc{font-size:13px !important;text-align:left !important;line-height:1.7 !important}
  .l-what-list{text-align:left !important;font-size:12px !important}
  .l-sec-h2{font-size:26px}
  .l-sec-label{font-size:9px}

  /* Problem */
  .l-prob-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}

  /* Demo */
  .l-demo-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}

  /* Steps */
  .l-step{padding:20px 0;gap:14px}
  .l-step-title{font-size:14px}
  .l-step-desc{font-size:13px}

  /* Pricing */
  .l-price-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}

  /* CTA */
  .l-cta-sec{padding:56px 16px}
  .l-cta-h2{font-size:26px}

  /* Footer */
  .l-footer{padding:28px 16px}
  .l-footer-inner{flex-direction:column;align-items:center;text-align:center;gap:14px}
  .l-footer-links{justify-content:center}
}

/* ══ SMALL MOBILE (≤380px) ═════════════════════════════════════ */
@media(max-width:380px){
  .l-hero-h1{font-size:24px}
  .l-sec-h2{font-size:22px}
  .l-cta-h2{font-size:22px}
  .l-prob-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}
}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="s-land" style="background:#EDECEA;color:#1A1916">

<!-- ══ NAV ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<nav class="l-nav">
  <div class="l-nav-logo" onclick="scrollTo(0,0)">
    <svg width="20" height="16" viewBox="0 0 22 17" fill="none">
      <rect x="5"  y="0"  width="12" height="3" rx="1.5" fill="#0C447C" opacity="0.45"/>
      <rect x="2"  y="6"  width="18" height="3" rx="1.5" fill="#0C447C" opacity="0.72"/>
      <rect x="0"  y="12" width="22" height="3" rx="1.5" fill="#0C447C"/>
    </svg>
    <span>Groundwork</span>
  </div>
  <div class="l-nav-links">
    <button class="l-nav-link active" onclick="lNav('home')">Home</button>
    <button class="l-nav-link" onclick="lNav('how')">How it works</button>
    <button class="l-nav-link" onclick="lNav('usecases')">Use cases</button>
    <button class="l-nav-link" onclick="lNav('pricing')">Pricing</button>
    <button class="l-nav-link" onclick="lNav('about')">About</button>
  </div>
  <div class="l-nav-ctas">
    <a class="l-btn-ghost" href={`${APP_URL}/auth?mode=member`}>Sign in</a>
    <a class="l-btn-solid" href={`${APP_URL}/start`}>Get started</a>
  </div>
</nav>

<!-- ══ PAGES ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<!-- HOME PAGE -->
<div id="lp-home">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <div class="l-hero">
    <svg class="l-net" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
      <defs><pattern id="lgrid" width="48" height="48" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
        <path d="M 48 0 L 0 0 0 48" fill="none" stroke="#93C5FD" stroke-width="0.4"/>
      </pattern></defs>
      <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#lgrid)" opacity="0.8"/>
      <circle cx="140" cy="90"  r="2" fill="#93C5FD" opacity="0.4"/>
      <circle cx="300" cy="60"  r="2" fill="#93C5FD" opacity="0.35"/>
      <circle cx="480" cy="130" r="2" fill="#5DCAA5" opacity="0.3"/>
      <circle cx="640" cy="80"  r="2" fill="#93C5FD" opacity="0.25"/>
      <circle cx="800" cy="140" r="2" fill="#5DCAA5" opacity="0.2"/>
      <line x1="140" y1="90" x2="300" y2="60" stroke="#93C5FD" stroke-width="0.5" opacity="0.15"/>
      <line x1="300" y1="60" x2="480" y2="130" stroke="#93C5FD" stroke-width="0.5" opacity="0.13"/>
      <line x1="480" y1="130" x2="640" y2="80" stroke="#5DCAA5" stroke-width="0.5" opacity="0.12"/>
      <line x1="640" y1="80" x2="800" y2="140" stroke="#93C5FD" stroke-width="0.5" opacity="0.1"/>
    </svg>
    <div class="l-hero-inner" style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;width:100%;padding:36px 20px 34px">
      <div class="l-hero-two">

        <!-- LEFT: who we are, what we promise, and why it matters. -->
        <div>
          <!-- The eyebrow slot, back in use. It held "Work alignment
               intelligence", which named a category rather than making a
               promise. -->
          <div class="l-hero-label">Know what's really happening</div>
          <h1 class="l-hero-h1" style="min-height:auto;line-height:1.12;letter-spacing:-.025em;margin:0 0 14px;font-size:38px">Keep your team aligned and moving forward by finally hearing everyone.</h1>

          <!-- Two variants rotate here. A third, "By finally hearing everyone.",
               was dropped when the headline absorbed it word for word.
               MEASUREMENT IS NOT WIRED: no analytics on this site and the one
               endpoint it can reach takes a fixed enum with nowhere to record a
               variant. This rotates; it does not yet report. -->
          <p id="l-subhead" class="l-hero-prose" style="font-size:17px;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.86);font-weight:500;line-height:1.5;margin:0 0 24px;max-width:460px">Even when you're not in the room.</p>
          <script is:inline>
            (function () {
              var VARIANTS = [
                "Even when you're not in the room.",
                "Without relying on the loudest voice in the room.",
              ];
              try {
                var k = 'gw_subhead_variant';
                var v = localStorage.getItem(k);
                if (v === null || +v >= VARIANTS.length) { v = String(Math.floor(Math.random() * VARIANTS.length)); localStorage.setItem(k, v); }
                var el = document.getElementById('l-subhead');
                if (el && VARIANTS[+v]) el.textContent = VARIANTS[+v];
              } catch (e) { /* no storage: the server-rendered first variant stands */ }
            })();
          </script>

          <div class="l-hero-problem-block l-hero-prose" style="max-width:460px">
            <p class="l-hero-problem" style="font-size:15px;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.66);line-height:1.75;margin:0 0 12px">Meetings only tell you part of the story. Updates get filtered. People remember things differently.</p>
            <p class="l-hero-problem" style="font-size:15px;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.66);line-height:1.75;margin:0">By the time you find out, you have often already lost your best people, hired wrong, missed a goal, or spent weeks solving the wrong problem.</p>
          </div>
          <!-- "Not by watching your team, but by finally hearing all of them."
               was here and is deleted. It was written as the continuation of a
               headline that no longer exists: under "Know what's really
               happening." it completed the thought, and under "Keep your team
               aligned and moving forward by finally hearing everyone." it
               restates the end of a sentence three lines above it.

               The anti-surveillance point it carried is made twice more further
               down, in the callout and in "What Groundwork is not", so nothing is
               lost by it going. -->

          <div class="l-hero-btns" id="l-getstarted-wrap">
            <a class="l-hero-btn-p l-cta-pulse" href={`${APP_URL}/start`} style="padding:14px 28px;font-size:15px">Start your first Ground</a>
            <a class="l-hero-btn-g" href={`${APP_URL}/auth`} style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.08);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15)">Sign in</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- RIGHT: what you actually do. -->
        <div>
          <div class="l-sw-lead">Start well</div>
          <div class="l-startwell">
            <div class="l-sw-step"><span class="l-sw-n">1</span><p>Create a Ground by setting the context, objectives and expectations.</p></div>
            <div class="l-sw-step"><span class="l-sw-n">2</span><p>Everyone involved checks in privately as the work progresses.</p></div>
            <div class="l-sw-step"><span class="l-sw-n">3</span><p>Check-ins turn into clear reports that show where things stand and what to do next.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <!-- WHAT GROUNDWORK DOES -->
  <div class="l-sec l-fade" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-bottom:1px solid #E2E0DB">
    <div class="l-wrap-sm">
      <p class="l-sec-label">What Groundwork does</p>
      <h2 class="l-sec-h2" style="margin-bottom:14px">It shows you what meetings miss.</h2>
      <!-- 6a: the signature sentence, moved down out of the hero rather than
           cut. It was competing with the headline up there; here it opens the
           section that explains the mechanism. -->
      <p style="font-size:17px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:26px;font-weight:500">Every person gives their own independent account. Groundwork compares every account and reveals the differences no meeting can.</p>
      <!-- B3: the independent account leads. It is the actual innovation and
           the reason everything downstream is worth reading, so it goes first
           rather than third. B4: headings say what you get, not what it does. -->
      <div class="l-fx-grid">
        <div class="l-fx l-fx-lead">
          <h3>Everyone answers on their own, in private</h3>
          <p>Nobody's account gets shaped by the loudest person in the room, or by what they think the manager wants to hear. That independence is what makes the comparison worth anything.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="l-fx">
          <h3>See where people quietly disagree</h3>
          <p>It lines up every account and shows where they match, where they don't, and whether the work is actually being delivered, while there is still time to do something about it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="l-fx">
          <h3>Catch problems before they become expensive</h3>
          <p>Each check-in is compared against what was agreed at the start, what was said before, and what everyone else is saying, so changing expectations, unclear ownership, and growing risk show up while you can still act.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="l-fx">
          <h3>It pushes past vague updates</h3>
          <p>Instead of accepting "things are going well", it asks for specifics, examples, and evidence.</p>
        </div>
        <!-- SAID AS A PRODUCT CLAIM, NOT LEFT AS A DETAIL OF AN EXAMPLE.
             The private guide was only visible on the page as a small card under
             one of the sample reports, so somebody deciding whether to buy this
             could not tell it existed as a feature at all. It is one of the two
             or three most valuable things here: every person gets coached, every
             time, and it costs the person running the ground nothing.

             With this card there are four followers under the lead, so the grid
             is a clean two-by-two and the odd-one-out rule that used to stretch
             the last card across the row is no longer needed. -->
        <div class="l-fx">
          <h3>Everyone gets their own quiet coaching</h3>
          <p>Each time a report lands, every person in the Ground gets a short private note drawn only from their own record: a way to open the conversation, a question worth carrying, and one thing somebody else raised that is worth taking seriously. Nobody reads anybody else's, including you.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- MOVED INSIDE "WHAT GROUNDWORK DOES", where it belongs.
           It sat in a section of its own after the sample reports, which made
           the strongest trust line on the page a footnote to the evidence
           rather than the closing statement of the mechanism it explains. A
           reader has the privacy question the moment they understand that
           everybody gives an independent account, which is one paragraph up. -->
  <!-- B11: ONE highlighted callout, and only one. The sentence that explains
       WHY privacy produces value is the strongest trust-builder on the page, so
       it gets its own frame rather than a buried line. Deliberately not stacked
       with more reassurance: a wall of defensiveness loses the reader before
       they understand the product. The fuller "what it is not" detail sits
       lower, for the person who scrolls looking for it. -->
      <div style="background:#EEF4FB;border:1px solid #C5D9EF;border-left:3px solid #0C447C;border-radius:10px;padding:22px 24px">
        <!-- "NEVER SHARES INDIVIDUAL ANSWERS" WAS NOT TRUE, and this was the
             loudest place on the page saying it.

             Checked against a real eight-session report for six people. Its
             divergences carry direct quotes, with the session they came from:

               Lead (Session 1): "Everybody knows their part. Ejiro has data,
                                  Maureen has product, Eric has infrastructure."

             Individual answers ARE shared. What is never shared is anybody's
             account of somebody ELSE, and that is the promise people actually
             need - it is the difference between a record and a report card. A
             false claim standing next to the real one makes both read as
             marketing, so the real one now stands on its own. -->
        <p style="font-size:17px;color:#0A1628;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 14px;font-weight:500">What you say about your own work can appear, in your own words. What you say about anybody else never does, and the report never says who said what about whom. That is why people tell the truth in it, and why the reports are worth reading.</p>
        <!-- VERIFIED BEFORE IT WENT ON A PUBLIC PAGE, line by line.
             "no screen anywhere that shows one" - the transcript endpoint is
             owner-only, and the platform-admin support view carries a documented
             hard boundary that excludes ConversationTurn content, RecordEntry
             content, every Report content field, LeadContextNote text and
             document contents. Its test poisons the query with all of them to
             prove none leaks.
             "read and synthesized by a language model, not by a person" - true.
             "they cannot request an individual's check-in" - true, there is no
             such route.

             ONE SENTENCE CHANGED, and it is worth saying why rather than doing it
             quietly. "Your data is private, including from us" reads as an
             absolute, and turns sit unencrypted in Postgres while transcripts go
             to Google for processing - so anybody with database access can read
             them and the sentence would not survive one technical question. The
             version here says the same thing in the form that is checkable, and
             it is your own stronger line: there is no screen, including ours.
             G41 is the work that would make the absolute version true. -->
        <p style="font-size:15px;color:#12314F;line-height:1.7;margin:0;font-weight:400">Nobody can speak for you. Your check-in is read and synthesized by a language model, not by a person: not by us, and not by whoever brought your team here. Leaders get the report. They cannot request an individual's check-in, and there is no screen anywhere that shows one, including ours.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <!-- THE SAMPLES SIT AFTER "WHAT GROUNDWORK DOES", NOT BEFORE IT.
       They were directly under the hero on the argument that people believe an
       example faster than a claim. True, and it put three dense reports in front
       of somebody who did not yet know what a Ground was, so the thing they were
       an example OF had not been said yet.

       Problem, mechanism, evidence. The reader now arrives here already knowing
       that everybody gives an independent account and that the report is the
       comparison - which is the sentence that makes these panels legible.

       EVERY ELEMENT IS REAL OUTPUT. Re-verified against the live report for a
       seven-person Q3 ground at the time of writing: divergence topic, three
       positions, the closing question and the agreements count are all fields
       the engine produces and populated. Nothing here is invented, and nothing
       richer than the real thing is shown. Names are neutralised because this
       is example data, not because the product hides them - a real report does
       attribute each position to the person who holds it. It ends on a
       QUESTION, which is what the engine produces; it does not issue
       instructions. -->
  <div class="l-sec l-fade" style="background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #E2E0DB">
    <div class="l-wrap-sm">
      <p class="l-sec-label">What a report looks like</p>
      <!-- TWO SITUATIONS, NOT ONE. A single sample answers "what does this look
           like" only for the person whose situation happens to match it. -->
      <div class="l-case-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Example reports">
        <button class="l-case-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-case="0">A sales team</button>
        <button class="l-case-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-case="1">Onboarding a new chief executive</button>
        <button class="l-case-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-case="2">Onboarding a whole cohort</button>
        <button class="l-case-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-case="3">A new hire in probation period</button>
      </div>
      <div style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--gw-muted,#9B9590);margin-bottom:14px">Scroll sideways for the other one.</div>

      <div class="l-cases" id="l-cases">
        <div class="l-case">
      <h2 class="l-sec-h2" style="margin-bottom:10px">Every pipeline review said the quarter was on track.</h2>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:8px">Eight people on the same sales team gave their own account, separately. Here is what came back.</p>
      <!-- THIS SAID "IT NEVER QUOTES ANYONE" AND THAT IS NOT TRUE.
           Checked against a real eight-session report for six people, generated
           the day this was written. Its divergences carry direct quotes with the
           session they came from:

             Lead (Session 1): "Everybody knows their part. Ejiro has data,
                                Maureen has product, Eric has infrastructure."
             Data (Session 4): "Maureen has been writing her own version of the
                                appointment table."

           The half of the sentence that IS true is the half that matters: it
           never says who said what ABOUT anybody else. Your own words can appear;
           somebody else's opinion of you cannot. Saying the first part wrongly
           was worse than useless, because the promise people actually need is the
           second one and a false claim next to it makes both look like marketing. -->
      <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:22px">An extract from a shared report, as the person running it sees it. Everyone in the Ground reads the same one. It quotes what people said about their own work, and it never says who said what about anybody else.</p>

      <!-- A SALES TEAM, BECAUSE EVERYBODY HAS ONE AND EVERYBODY RECOGNISES IT.
           The earlier version used a product launch. It was concrete but narrow:
           you had to be shipping software for it to be your problem. Selling is
           the thing every company is doing, and the failure it shows here is the
           one every sales lead has lived through - seven people selling a
           slightly different thing to a slightly different buyer, all of it
           landing in one pipeline number that looks fine.

           WHAT PEOPLE DID COMES FIRST, and for a sales team that means activity
           and outcomes: meetings, proposals, pilots, a renewal, a close. Then the
           small things that changed and are working, because that is how a team
           actually gets better at selling and it is invisible in a CRM.

           All four of these are things the product computes and the panel was not
           showing: quickRead in board.service.ts, evidenceCount per person in
           reads.ts, the alignment enum in entry.service.ts, and specificityLevel
           on every check-in.

           IT NAMES PEOPLE BECAUSE THE REAL REPORT DOES - namesVisibleTo is
           `viewerIsLead || isSelf || isLead`. What is never shown, here or in the
           product, is anything anybody said ABOUT somebody else. -->
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #D8D5CF;border-radius:12px;overflow:hidden;box-shadow:0 4px 16px rgba(26,25,22,.07)">

        <div style="background:#0A1628;padding:13px 22px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">
          <span style="font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;color:#fff;letter-spacing:-.01em">New business, this quarter &middot; shared report</span>
          <span style="font-size:11px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.55);letter-spacing:.04em">8 accounts &middot; check-in 3 of 6</span>
        </div>

        <div style="background:#FBF6F6;border-bottom:1px solid #EFE2E2;padding:16px 22px">
          <p style="font-size:16.5px;font-weight:700;color:#791F1F;line-height:1.45;margin:0 0 6px;letter-spacing:-.01em">Nine of the twelve live deals are with somebody who cannot sign, at a tenth of the size the quarter was planned on.</p>
          <p style="font-size:13px;color:#8A5C5C;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Surfaced at check-in 3 of 6. The next time it would have come up on its own is the end-of-quarter number, when the meeting count will still look healthy.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="l-qr">
          <div><div class="k">Aligned?</div><div class="v" style="color:#8A5C1A">2 open</div><div class="s">differences between accounts</div></div>
          <div><div class="k">Moving?</div><div class="v" style="color:#085041">+14</div><div class="s">across targets this session</div></div>
          <div><div class="k">Stuck?</div><div class="v" style="color:#8A5C1A">1 blocked</div><div class="s">waiting on someone else</div></div>
          <div><div class="k">Solid?</div><div class="v" style="color:#085041">good</div><div class="s">64% document-backed</div></div>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:20px 22px 6px">
          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px">What moved in the last three weeks</div>
          <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:10px">Each person's own account of their own work. Nobody is describing anybody else.</div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Ada</div><div class="did">Closed Microdot Inc. First deal over a hundred thousand, and the reference call is booked.</div><span class="ev">2 documents</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Dan</div><div class="did">Eleven first meetings, four second meetings, two proposals out. Six of the eleven were with Halden, who has not put anyone in the room who can sign.</div><span class="ev">4 documents</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Rachel</div><div class="did">Six first meetings and three pilots agreed. All three start next month.</div><span class="ev">3 documents</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Naomi</div><div class="did">Renewed Vanta at a higher tier and lost nothing this quarter. Of the three weeks, most went on renewal paperwork, contract admin and tidying the CRM. Two customer conversations in total.</div><span class="ev">2 documents</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Tom</div><div class="did">Wrote the objection sheet the rest of the team is now using on calls.</div><span class="ev">3 documents</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Yusuf</div><div class="did">Nineteen first meetings. No second meetings yet.</div><span class="ev">1 document</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Kofi</div><div class="did">New CRM shipped in week two. Team is on it.</div><span class="ev ev-none">no evidence yet</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Ellie</div><div class="did">Nothing specific named yet across three check-ins.</div><span class="ev ev-none">no evidence yet</span></div>
        </div>

        <!-- THE ONE THING A SINGLE ACCOUNT CAN NEVER CATCH.
             Somebody reports a thing as done. Everybody else is still working
             around the fact that it is not. Neither side is lying and neither
             side knows: Kofi shipped something and believes it is in use; the
             reps are entering everything twice and assume that is the plan for
             now. It stays invisible until two accounts of the same thing are put
             next to each other, which is the entire mechanism of this product and
             the clearest possible demonstration of it.

             THE PRODUCT WILL NOT SAY WHO IS RIGHT, and neither does this panel.
             It shows both accounts and names the conversation. That restraint is
             the difference between a tool people tell the truth to and a tool
             people learn to manage - and the moment it declared a winner here,
             nobody would ever report anything as done again. -->
        <div style="padding:14px 22px 4px">
          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:10px">Two accounts of the same thing, side by side</div>
          <div class="l-told" style="margin-bottom:10px">
            <div style="background:#F3F6FA;border:1px solid #D6E3F2;border-radius:7px;padding:13px 15px">
              <p style="font-size:14px;color:#12314F;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 8px">"The new CRM went live in week two. The team is on it."</p>
              <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#0C447C;background:#E1EBF7;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Kofi, sales operations</span>
            </div>
            <div style="background:#FBF6F6;border:1px solid #EFDDDD;border-radius:7px;padding:13px 15px">
              <p style="font-size:14px;color:#5A2020;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 8px">Still working in the old sheet and entering everything twice. Three of them describe it as the reason their admin has doubled.</p>
              <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#791F1F;background:#F7E3E3;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Dan, Rachel, Naomi and Yusuf</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:13px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 4px">Both accounts are on record and neither person knew about the other. Nothing here decides who is right, and the report will not: it is a conversation that was otherwise going to happen at the end of the quarter.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- CLOSE, SIZE AND MOVEMENT, AGAINST WHAT WAS ACTUALLY PLANNED.
             The thing a chief executive recognises fastest, and the one a busy
             pipeline hides best: the team IS moving, and it is moving somewhere
             else. Meetings up, activity up, everybody working - into deals a
             tenth of the size, with people who cannot sign.

             Nothing here is about a person. It is the shape of the quarter set
             against the shape that was agreed, which is a fact about the work and
             something the whole team can act on.

             AHEAD OF THE PRODUCT, and logged with G44. The engine holds the
             targets, the movement against them and every account behind those
             numbers; it does not yet lay the plan and the actual side by side
             like this. It should. -->
        <div style="padding:14px 22px 4px">
          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:10px">Where the movement is, against where we said we were going</div>
          <div class="l-told" style="margin-bottom:10px">
            <div style="background:#F3F6FA;border:1px solid #D6E3F2;border-radius:7px;padding:13px 15px">
              <p style="font-size:14px;color:#12314F;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 8px">Operators running two hundred vehicles or more. Deals around sixty thousand. Two closed a quarter.</p>
              <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#0C447C;background:#E1EBF7;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">what we agreed in week one</span>
            </div>
            <div style="background:#FBF6F6;border:1px solid #EFDDDD;border-radius:7px;padding:13px 15px">
              <p style="font-size:14px;color:#5A2020;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 8px">Twelve deals live. Nine under ten thousand, all with IT managers. One with an operations lead. One closed.</p>
              <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#791F1F;background:#F7E3E3;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">where the quarter actually is</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#1A1916;font-weight:600;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 4px">The team is moving. It is moving somewhere else.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- WHAT ONE PERSON COULD DO DIFFERENTLY DOES NOT BELONG IN A DOCUMENT
             SEVEN COLLEAGUES READ.
             This block used to carry "maybe worth a conversation" rows naming
             individuals - Yusuf's nineteen meetings that went nowhere, Tom's two
             quiet deals, Naomi's three weeks of admin - inside the SHARED report.
             Every one of them was factual and every one of them would have been
             humiliating, because the whole team reads this.

             That was me collapsing three surfaces into one. The product keeps
             them apart on purpose:

               the shared report      everyone     what is happening to the WORK
               the board              the lead     reads about people, with reasons
               the post-report guide  you alone    what you might do differently

             postReportGuide is keyed by participant id and returned only to that
             participant - not to the other six, and not to the lead either. So
             the concern rows come out of the shared panel entirely and appear
             below it, outside the document, as the private thing they are.

             WHAT STAYS SHARED IS WHAT IS GENEROUS. A small change somebody made
             that is working is worth the whole team seeing, and it is the one
             kind of personal detail that costs nobody anything to have read. -->
        <div style="padding:14px 22px 4px">
          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px">Small changes that are working</div>
          <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:10px">Named by the person who made them, across three check-ins. Nothing here is a score and nobody is ranked against anybody else.</div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Dan</div><div class="did">Started opening with the cost line instead of the feature list in week two. First meetings turning into second meetings went from one in five to one in three.</div><span class="ev">worth copying</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Rachel</div><div class="did">Stopped sending the deck before the call. Fewer people cancel.</div><span class="ev">worth copying</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Rachel</div><div class="did">Books the next meeting before leaving the first one. Six of six first meetings already have a second date.</div><span class="ev">worth copying</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Dan</div><div class="did">Writes every call up the same day. His is the only account where the numbers can be checked against something.</div><span class="ev">worth copying</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Ada</div><div class="did">Asks for the decision date on the first call. Microdot named a date in week two and held it.</div><span class="ev">worth copying</span></div>
          <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Tom</div><div class="did">Wrote down the four objections that come up every time. Three people are using it.</div><span class="ev">worth copying</span></div>
        </div>

        <div style="padding:14px 22px 18px">

          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:10px">Where the summary and the record differ</div>
          <div class="l-told" style="margin-bottom:10px">
            <div style="background:#F3F6FA;border:1px solid #D6E3F2;border-radius:7px;padding:13px 15px">
              <p style="font-size:14px;color:#12314F;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 8px">"Good quarter. Everyone is busy, the meeting numbers are up, and we are ahead on pipeline."</p>
              <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#0C447C;background:#E1EBF7;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Ada's summary, week 6</span>
            </div>
            <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-radius:7px;padding:13px 15px">
              <p style="font-size:14px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 8px">Three different buyers, two different products, and one person's meetings are not moving at all.</p>
              <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#6B6560;background:#EDEBE7;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">what the other seven accounts hold</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:13px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 24px">Nobody was wrong. Ada was reporting what had reached her.</p>

          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:6px">Who we each think we are selling to</div>
          <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#1A1916;margin-bottom:12px;letter-spacing:-.01em">The buyer, in each person's own words</div>
          <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #0C447C;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:7px">
            <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Heads of operations at logistics firms running two hundred vehicles or more. They hold the budget.</p>
            <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#0C447C;background:#E7EFF8;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Ada</span>
          </div>
          <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #9B9590;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:7px">
            <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Whoever takes the meeting. Mostly IT managers, because they answer.</p>
            <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#6B6560;background:#EDEBE7;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Dan and Yusuf</span>
          </div>
          <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #8A5C1A;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:0">
            <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Finance leads, because the pitch that lands is the cost saving.</p>
            <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#8A5C1A;background:#FBF1E0;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Rachel</span>
          </div>

          <div style="height:20px"></div>

          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:6px">Waiting for an owner</div>
          <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#1A1916;margin-bottom:12px;letter-spacing:-.01em">Turning a finished pilot into a paid contract</div>
          <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #9B9590;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:7px">
            <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Understood it to go back to the person who sold the pilot, and has not asked.</p>
            <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#6B6560;background:#EDEBE7;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Dan, Naomi and Yusuf</span>
          </div>
          <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #0C447C;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:0">
            <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Has three pilots starting next month and no agreed step after them.</p>
            <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#0C447C;background:#E7EFF8;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Rachel</span>
          </div>

          <p style="font-size:14px;color:#1A1916;font-weight:600;line-height:1.6;margin:14px 0 24px;padding-left:2px">It has come up three times and has not been given to anyone yet.</p>

          <div style="background:#EEF4FB;border:1px solid #C9DDF3;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px">
            <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:7px">The one thing to settle first</div>
            <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#12314F;line-height:1.65;margin:0">Which buyer can actually sign, and who owns turning a finished pilot into a paid contract?</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div style="border-top:1px solid #EDEBE7;background:#FAF9F7;padding:14px 22px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">
          <span style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.5;flex:1;min-width:220px">Everyone agrees on: the price list, the pilot length, and the number the team is aiming at.</span>
          <span style="font-size:11.5px;font-weight:700;color:#0C447C;background:#E7EFF8;border:1px solid #C9DDF3;border-radius:20px;padding:5px 12px;white-space:nowrap">Emerging &middot; 3 settled, 2 open</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- THE PRIVATE HALF, OUTSIDE THE PANEL ON PURPOSE.
           The boundary between what the team reads and what only you read is the
           most important line in this product, and showing it is worth more than
           any sentence claiming it.

           SHAPED LIKE THE REAL THING. postReportGuide produces exactly three
           fields - an opening line, a question to carry, and one thing somebody
           else raised that is worth taking seriously - and its prompt forbids
           naming or quoting anyone even here, in a document only one person ever
           sees. So these say "the other view" and "someone else", the way the
           product does. -->
      <div style="max-width:640px;margin:18px 0 0;background:#FAF9F7;border:1px dashed #C9C5BE;border-radius:10px;padding:16px 18px">
        <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:10px">What only Yusuf sees</div>

        <!-- SAY WHAT IT IS, NOT THAT IT IS SECRET.
             The heading read "and one note that nobody else sees", which is a
             claim about absence and says nothing about what the thing does. Two
             people who matter were left guessing: a chief executive could not
             tell that their whole team is quietly being coached one by one, and
             a team member could not tell that the coaching is THEIRS rather than
             a report about them. Both of those are the point, so both are said. -->
        <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 12px">Everyone in the Ground gets one of these each time a report lands, drawn only from their own record. Nobody reads anybody else's, and neither does the person running it.</p>
        <div style="margin-bottom:11px">
          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something you could say</div>
          <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">"I am getting first meetings and they are not turning into second ones, and I do not think I have worked out yet what makes the difference."</p>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-bottom:11px">
          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something to ask</div>
          <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">When one of these does move on, what happened in that meeting that is not happening in mine?</p>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-bottom:12px">
          <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something to take seriously</div>
          <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Somebody else here is asking for the next date inside the first meeting rather than afterwards. Worth trying on the next one, even if it feels early.</p>
        </div>

        <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Yusuf's own note, in his own copy of the report. The other seven do not see it. Neither does the person running the ground.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:17px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;font-weight:600;margin:24px 0 0">Everyone was working hard, on their own version of the customer. Nobody on that team could have told you this, because no one of them could see it.</p>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-top:16px">Nobody was withholding anything. Each person answered honestly about their own work. The gap only appears when the accounts are put side by side.</p>
      <!-- Said plainly, because somebody whose manager forwarded them this page
           is reading it to work out what it means for them. -->
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-top:16px">If you are one of the eight, this is your report too. Same report, same moment. It is how the thing that is working for you gets shared with everyone else, and how the thing you have been raising for three weeks finally gets a name against it.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- CASE TWO: A PERSON BEING ONBOARDED, AND WHAT CHANGED BETWEEN THEM.
             The sales report is a team at a moment. This one is one person over
             time, which is the other half of what this product does and the half
             a probation period actually needs: where they started apart, where
             it has already closed, and what is still read differently on day 62
             of 90.

             It is deliberately NOT a disaster. Most onboardings are going fine
             with two live misunderstandings in them, and a page where every
             sample is a catastrophe teaches people that the report only shows up
             when something is wrong - which is exactly when they will stop
             telling it the truth.

             The realignment block is the one that could not exist without this
             product. A gap that closed, with the session it closed in, from four
             accounts none of which could see the others. And it is faithful:
             Ground 2's real report carries exactly this in `agreements` - "a
             period of confusion where two people built the same appointment
             table was resolved, clarifying ownership".

             Kaya Labs, the sites and the people are invented. -->
        <div class="l-case">
          <h2 class="l-sec-h2" style="margin-bottom:10px">Day 62 of a 90 day handover.</h2>
          <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:8px">A new chief executive at Kaya Labs, a franchise of eleven small HIV research sites. Her board chair, her lab network director and her funder lead each gave their own account, separately.</p>
          <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:22px">An extract from a shared report, as the person running it sees it. Everyone in the Ground reads the same one.</p>

          <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #D8D5CF;border-radius:12px;overflow:hidden;box-shadow:0 4px 16px rgba(26,25,22,.07)">

            <div style="background:#0A1628;padding:13px 22px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">
              <span style="font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;color:#fff;letter-spacing:-.01em">Nomsa, first 90 days &middot; shared report</span>
              <span style="font-size:11px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.55);letter-spacing:.04em">4 accounts &middot; check-in 4 of 6</span>
            </div>

            <div style="background:#F2F9F6;border-bottom:1px solid #CFE7DC;padding:16px 22px">
              <p style="font-size:16.5px;font-weight:700;color:#085041;line-height:1.45;margin:0 0 6px;letter-spacing:-.01em">Two of the three things you started apart on have closed. The third is about money and has not moved.</p>
              <p style="font-size:13px;color:#3A7A60;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Both closed in the session after they surfaced. Neither was raised in a meeting first.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="l-qr">
              <div><div class="k">Aligned?</div><div class="v" style="color:#8A5C1A">1 open</div><div class="s">down from three</div></div>
              <div><div class="k">Moving?</div><div class="v" style="color:#085041">+7</div><div class="s">across targets this session</div></div>
              <div><div class="k">Stuck?</div><div class="v" style="color:#085041">clear</div><div class="s">nothing hard-blocked</div></div>
              <div><div class="k">Solid?</div><div class="v" style="color:#085041">good</div><div class="s">83% document-backed</div></div>
            </div>

            <div style="padding:20px 22px 6px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#085041;margin-bottom:4px">Where you have already realigned</div>
              <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">A gap that has closed, with the session it closed in.</div>

              <div style="border:1px solid #CFE7DC;background:#F7FBF9;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;margin-bottom:9px">
                <div style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:#1A1916;margin-bottom:8px">What running Kaya Labs well means</div>
                <div class="l-told" style="margin-bottom:8px">
                  <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-radius:7px;padding:11px 13px">
                    <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 6px">Nomsa: eleven sites open by year one. Helen: sample integrity holding at every existing site.</p>
                    <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;color:#8A5C1A;background:#FBF1E0;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 9px">session 1, apart</span>
                  </div>
                  <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #CFE7DC;border-radius:7px;padding:11px 13px">
                    <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 6px">No new site until the four running sites hold integrity above 98 per cent for a full quarter. Both describe it that way now.</p>
                    <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;color:#085041;background:#DFF1EA;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 9px">session 3, agreed</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div style="border:1px solid #CFE7DC;background:#F7FBF9;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;margin-bottom:16px">
                <div style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:#1A1916;margin-bottom:8px">Who signs off capital for a new site</div>
                <div class="l-told" style="margin-bottom:8px">
                  <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-radius:7px;padding:11px 13px">
                    <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 6px">Nomsa understood site capital to sit with her, and had begun costing two locations.</p>
                    <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;color:#8A5C1A;background:#FBF1E0;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 9px">session 2, apart</span>
                  </div>
                  <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #CFE7DC;border-radius:7px;padding:11px 13px">
                    <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 6px">It sits with the board. She kept the costings and they are now the paper the board will decide from.</p>
                    <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;color:#085041;background:#DFF1EA;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 9px">session 3, agreed</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:6px">Still read differently</div>
              <div style="font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#1A1916;margin-bottom:12px;letter-spacing:-.01em">What the funder is owed, and how often</div>
              <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #9B9590;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:7px">
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">A monthly summary across the network is what the funder wants, and it has gone out every month.</p>
                <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#6B6560;background:#EDEBE7;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Nomsa</span>
              </div>
              <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #8A5C1A;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:14px">
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">The grant terms ask for per-site data every month, not a network summary. Two sites have filed nothing since March.</p>
                <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#8A5C1A;background:#FBF1E0;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Sarah and Kwame</span>
              </div>
              <p style="font-size:13px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 20px">Nobody was wrong. Nomsa was reporting against what she had been handed, and the grant terms were not in it.</p>

              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px">What she has delivered, session by session</div>
              <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:10px">Her own account of her own work, in her own words.</div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Sessions 1&ndash;2</div><div class="did">Visited all four running sites. Found the integrity gap at the Bulawayo site herself and paused its intake.</div><span class="ev">3 documents</span></div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Session 3</div><div class="did">Costed two candidate locations. Wrote the first network-wide protocol for sample handling.</div><span class="ev">4 documents</span></div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Session 4</div><div class="did">Integrity at Bulawayo back above 98 per cent. Two lab leads recruited. Funder call taken alone for the first time.</div><span class="ev">2 documents</span></div>

              <div style="background:#EEF4FB;border:1px solid #C9DDF3;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;margin-top:18px">
                <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:7px">The one thing to settle first</div>
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#12314F;line-height:1.65;margin:0">What exactly does the grant require each month, per site rather than across the network, and who is accountable for the two sites that have filed nothing since March?</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div style="border-top:1px solid #EDEBE7;background:#FAF9F7;padding:14px 22px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">
              <span style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.5;flex:1;min-width:220px">Everyone agrees on: the integrity threshold, the order sites open in, and who runs the funder relationship.</span>
              <span style="font-size:11.5px;font-weight:700;color:#0C447C;background:#E7EFF8;border:1px solid #C9DDF3;border-radius:20px;padding:5px 12px;white-space:nowrap">Emerging &middot; 3 settled, 1 open</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- THE PRIVATE HALF, OUTSIDE THE PANEL ON PURPOSE.
               The boundary between what the team reads and what only you read is the
               most important line in this product, and showing it is worth more than
