                 tell that their whole team is quietly being coached one by one, and
                 a team member could not tell that the coaching is THEIRS rather than
                 a report about them. Both of those are the point, so both are said. -->
            <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 12px">Everyone in the Ground gets one of these each time a report lands, drawn only from their own record. Nobody reads anybody else's, and neither does the person running it.</p>
            <div style="margin-bottom:11px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something you could say</div>
              <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">"I have been sending a monthly summary across the network, and I am no longer sure that is what the grant actually asks for."</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:11px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something to ask</div>
              <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">What exactly is owed each month, site by site rather than across the network, and which parts of it am I not sending?</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:12px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something to take seriously</div>
              <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Someone closer to the funding reads the terms as per-site. That is worth checking before the next filing rather than after it.</p>
            </div>

            <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Nomsa's own note, in her own copy of the report. The board chair does not see it.</p>
          </div>

          <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:17px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;font-weight:600;margin:24px 0 0">Two misunderstandings, both caught in the session after they appeared. On the old path the first one anybody hears about either is the review at day ninety.</p>
          <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-top:16px">Nobody was hiding anything and nobody was underperforming. A new chief executive was working from the handover she was given, and three people around her each held a piece of what was missing from it.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- CASE THREE: A COHORT, WHERE THE FINDING IS ABOUT THE ONBOARDING.
             Fourteen people hired into the same role in the same month, working
             in fourteen different districts. This is a different shape again,
             and the product already knows it is: peopleWorkTogether is false on
             a ground like this, and reads.ts says so out loud - "nobody else on
             this ground sees this person's work, so there is no second account
             to check any of this against."

             THE COUNT IS THE FINDING HERE, AND THAT IS A REAL TENSION.
             "Never count the accounts" is a rule this product enforces, because
             on shared work "four of the six said X" turns a headcount into a
             verdict. On a cohort it is the opposite: when the same wrong answer
             appears in nine independent accounts of the same induction, the count
             IS the diagnosis, and it points at the induction rather than at
             anybody in it.

             So this panel counts, deliberately, and says why. Logged in the fix
             plan, because countsAccounts() does not yet know the difference and
             would flag a correct cohort report as a violation.

             The districts, the programme and the people are invented. -->
        <div class="l-case">
          <h2 class="l-sec-h2" style="margin-bottom:10px">Fourteen people started the same job in the same month.</h2>
          <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:8px">New field officers on a twelve week probation, one per district. They have never met each other. Each gave their own account, separately, to the one programme manager they all report to.</p>
          <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:22px">An extract from a shared report, as the person running it sees it. Everyone in the Ground reads the same one.</p>

          <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #D8D5CF;border-radius:12px;overflow:hidden;box-shadow:0 4px 16px rgba(26,25,22,.07)">

            <div style="background:#0A1628;padding:13px 22px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">
              <span style="font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;color:#fff;letter-spacing:-.01em">Field officer intake, week 5 &middot; shared report</span>
              <span style="font-size:11px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.55);letter-spacing:.04em">14 accounts &middot; check-in 2 of 4</span>
            </div>

            <div style="background:#FBF6F6;border-bottom:1px solid #EFE2E2;padding:16px 22px">
              <p style="font-size:16.5px;font-weight:700;color:#791F1F;line-height:1.45;margin:0 0 6px;letter-spacing:-.01em">Nine of the fourteen described the escalation rule the same wrong way. That is the induction, not the people.</p>
              <p style="font-size:13px;color:#8A5C5C;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Fourteen accounts given separately, in fourteen districts, by people who have never met. One briefing produced all nine.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="l-qr">
              <div><div class="k">Aligned?</div><div class="v" style="color:#8A5C1A">2 open</div><div class="s">differences between accounts</div></div>
              <div><div class="k">Moving?</div><div class="v" style="color:#085041">+11</div><div class="s">across targets this session</div></div>
              <div><div class="k">Stuck?</div><div class="v" style="color:#8A5C1A">3 blocked</div><div class="s">all waiting on the same thing</div></div>
              <div><div class="k">Solid?</div><div class="v" style="color:#8A5C1A">thin</div><div class="s">29% document-backed</div></div>
            </div>

            <div style="padding:20px 22px 6px">
              <!-- The block that only a cohort produces. -->
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px">The same gap, in account after account</div>
              <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">Counted on purpose. On shared work a headcount is not evidence; across a cohort who have never spoken, it is the difference between one person misunderstanding and one briefing misteaching.</div>

              <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #791F1F;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:7px">
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">A stock-out goes in the weekly report. Nine accounts describe it that way. The policy is a phone call the same day, and three districts have been out of test kits for over a week.</p>
                <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#791F1F;background:#FBEBEB;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">9 of 14 &middot; taught, not guessed</span>
              </div>
              <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #8A5C1A;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:16px">
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Nobody can say who signs off a district budget variance. Eleven accounts name a different person or say they do not know.</p>
                <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#8A5C1A;background:#FBF1E0;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">11 of 14 &middot; never covered</span>
              </div>

              <!-- The limitation, stated rather than hidden. -->
              <div style="background:#F5F3EF;border:1px dashed #C9C5BE;border-radius:8px;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:18px">
                <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6B6560;margin-bottom:6px">What this report cannot tell you</div>
                <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.6;margin:0">These fourteen work in fourteen districts and none of them sees anyone else's work. So nothing here is corroborated, and somebody who did the work but describes it flatly reads exactly like somebody who did not. Every line below is one account, not a confirmed one.</p>
              </div>

              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px">Where the fourteen are not alike</div>
              <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:10px">Same start date, same briefing, same role.</div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Tendai</div><div class="did">Ran the first community session in week three and has referral numbers for it. The only account in the fourteen that names a figure anybody could check.</div><span class="ev">5 documents</span></div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Zainab</div><div class="did">Out of test kits since week two. Filed it in the weekly report as taught, and has heard nothing since.</div><span class="ev">2 documents</span></div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Kwabena</div><div class="did">Covering two districts because the neighbouring officer has not started. Nobody has told him when that ends.</div><span class="ev">1 document</span></div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Six others</div><div class="did">Describe the work in general terms across both check-ins, with nothing specific named yet.</div><span class="ev ev-none">no evidence yet</span></div>

              <div style="background:#EEF4FB;border:1px solid #C9DDF3;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;margin-top:18px">
                <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:7px">The one thing to settle first</div>
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#12314F;line-height:1.65;margin:0">What does a stock-out require, in one sentence every officer can repeat, and who do they call? The three districts out of kits are waiting on that answer, not on their own initiative.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div style="border-top:1px solid #EDEBE7;background:#FAF9F7;padding:14px 22px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">
              <span style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.5;flex:1;min-width:220px">Everyone agrees on: the district boundaries, the weekly reporting day, and what the role is for.</span>
              <span style="font-size:11.5px;font-weight:700;color:#8A5C1A;background:#FBF1E0;border:1px solid #F0DFC0;border-radius:20px;padding:5px 12px;white-space:nowrap">Mixed &middot; 3 settled, 2 open</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- THE PRIVATE HALF, OUTSIDE THE PANEL ON PURPOSE.
               The boundary between what the team reads and what only you read is the
               most important line in this product, and showing it is worth more than
               any sentence claiming it.

               SHAPED LIKE THE REAL THING. postReportGuide produces exactly three
               fields - an opening line, a question to carry, and one thing somebody
               else raised that is worth taking seriously - and its prompt forbids
               naming or quoting anyone even here, in a document only one person ever
               sees. So these say "the other view" and "someone else", the way the
               product does. -->
          <div style="max-width:640px;margin:18px 0 0;background:#FAF9F7;border:1px dashed #C9C5BE;border-radius:10px;padding:16px 18px">
            <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:10px">What only Zainab sees</div>

            <!-- SAY WHAT IT IS, NOT THAT IT IS SECRET.
                 The heading read "and one note that nobody else sees", which is a
                 claim about absence and says nothing about what the thing does. Two
                 people who matter were left guessing: a chief executive could not
                 tell that their whole team is quietly being coached one by one, and
                 a team member could not tell that the coaching is THEIRS rather than
                 a report about them. Both of those are the point, so both are said. -->
            <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 12px">Everyone in the Ground gets one of these each time a report lands, drawn only from their own record. Nobody reads anybody else's, and neither does the person running it.</p>
            <div style="margin-bottom:11px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something you could say</div>
              <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">"I have had no test kits since week two. I filed it the way I was taught to, and I do not think the way I was taught is reaching anyone."</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:11px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something to ask</div>
              <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">When something runs out, what is supposed to happen the same day, and who actually hears about it?</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:12px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something to take seriously</div>
              <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Others describe that rule the same way you do, so this is very unlikely to be something you got wrong on your own. Say it once more, out loud, to the person who can send kits.</p>
            </div>

            <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Zainab's own note, in her own copy of the report. The other thirteen do not see it, and neither does the programme manager.</p>
          </div>

          <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:17px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;font-weight:600;margin:24px 0 0">Nine people did not make the same mistake. One briefing did, fourteen times over, and only two of them had a way of finding that out.</p>
          <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-top:16px">The fix is an hour of rewriting an induction, and it arrives in week five instead of at the end of probation, when it would have arrived as fourteen separate performance conversations.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- CASE FOUR: A NEW HIRE, CHECKED AGAINST THEIR OWN ONBOARDING.
             The other three ask what people think and what they delivered. This
             one asks the question a probation actually turns on: is the work this
             person is doing pointed at the thing they were hired to do?

             Three things get set side by side, and none of them is an opinion:
             what the onboarding told them to do, what the record says they did,
             and what the ninety-day goal asks for. A new hire can be doing
             exactly what they were told and still be nowhere near the goal, and
             nothing in a normal review process shows that - it shows up as a
             disappointing month three.

             THIS ONE IS THE REAL FINDING FROM GROUND 1, reshaped and renamed. A
             twelve-session run produced exactly this: a manager whose definition
             of success was judgement, a hire measured on a ticket queue, and six
             weeks of somebody performing well against the only number anybody had
             given him. The report the engine wrote closed on it - "I was measured
             on a queue and judged on judgement, and only one of those was said".

             Names invented. The shape is not. -->
        <div class="l-case">
          <h2 class="l-sec-h2" style="margin-bottom:10px">Week seven of a ninety day probation.</h2>
          <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:8px">Tobi joined as a delivery lead and is seven weeks into a ninety day probation. His manager, a colleague who works alongside him, and Tobi each gave their own account, separately.</p>
          <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:22px">An extract from a shared report, as the person running it sees it. Everyone in the Ground reads the same one.</p>

          <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #D8D5CF;border-radius:12px;overflow:hidden;box-shadow:0 4px 16px rgba(26,25,22,.07)">

            <div style="background:#0A1628;padding:13px 22px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">
              <span style="font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;color:#fff;letter-spacing:-.01em">Tobi, first 90 days &middot; shared report</span>
              <span style="font-size:11px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.55);letter-spacing:.04em">3 accounts &middot; check-in 7 of 12</span>
            </div>

            <div style="background:#FBF6F6;border-bottom:1px solid #EFE2E2;padding:16px 22px">
              <p style="font-size:16.5px;font-weight:700;color:#791F1F;line-height:1.45;margin:0 0 6px;letter-spacing:-.01em">He is hitting the only target anybody gave him, and it is not the one he is being judged against.</p>
              <p style="font-size:13px;color:#8A5C5C;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Week seven of twelve. Nothing in the record has pointed at the ninety-day goal yet, and nothing has told him to.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="l-qr">
              <div><div class="k">Aligned?</div><div class="v" style="color:#8A5C1A">1 open</div><div class="s">differences between accounts</div></div>
              <div><div class="k">Moving?</div><div class="v" style="color:#085041">+18</div><div class="s">across targets this session</div></div>
              <div><div class="k">Stuck?</div><div class="v" style="color:#085041">clear</div><div class="s">nothing hard-blocked</div></div>
              <div><div class="k">Solid?</div><div class="v" style="color:#085041">good</div><div class="s">76% document-backed</div></div>
            </div>

            <div style="padding:20px 22px 6px">
              <!-- The block this case exists for. -->
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px">What he was onboarded to do, and what he has been doing</div>
              <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">His onboarding plan on one side, seven sessions of his own account on the other.</div>

              <div class="l-told" style="margin-bottom:9px">
                <div style="background:#F3F6FA;border:1px solid #D6E3F2;border-radius:7px;padding:13px 15px">
                  <p style="font-size:14px;color:#12314F;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 8px">Clear the support queue each week. Shadow two accounts. No direct client contact before month two.</p>
                  <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#0C447C;background:#E1EBF7;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">the onboarding plan, week 1</span>
                </div>
                <div style="background:#F2F9F6;border:1px solid #CFE7DC;border-radius:7px;padding:13px 15px">
                  <p style="font-size:14px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.55;margin:0 0 8px">Queue cleared every week, 31 tickets in the busiest. Shadowed two accounts. No client contact. Nothing late.</p>
                  <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#085041;background:#DFF1EA;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">what his account holds</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p style="font-size:14px;color:#085041;font-weight:600;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 10px;padding-left:2px">He has done exactly what the plan asked for, every week, and it is checkable.</p>
              <!-- AND HE IS SENIOR, WHICH CHANGES WHAT "DID WHAT HE WAS TOLD" IS
                   WORTH. At week one of a first job it is the whole answer. For
                   somebody hired to lead delivery it is half of one: the plan was
                   always going to run out before ninety days did, and noticing
                   that is part of the job rather than an extra.

                   Said as a fact about the record and pointed at BOTH of them.
                   Nobody told him the plan stopped at week four, and he did not
                   ask - and the second half only reads as fair because the first
                   half is there. A report that named only his silence would be a
                   performance note wearing a record's clothes. -->
              <div style="background:#FBF8F2;border:1px solid #F0E3CC;border-left:3px solid #8A5C1A;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:22px">
                <p style="font-size:14px;color:#5A4110;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 6px;font-weight:600">He was hired to lead delivery, and the plan ran out in week four.</p>
                <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#6B5A38;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Nothing in seven sessions has him asking what comes after the shadowing, proposing an account to take on, or naming something he needed to learn to get there. Nobody told him the plan ended either. At this level, noticing that the plan has run out is part of the work - and being given a plan that stops at week four is the other half of why it has not happened.</p>
              </div>

              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px">And what the ninety-day goal asks for</div>
              <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">Set at the start, by his manager, in her words.</div>

              <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #0C447C;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:7px">
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">"By ninety days I want to hand him a messy client problem and not think about it again. Owning at least one relationship end to end."</p>
                <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#0C447C;background:#E7EFF8;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Claire, week 1</span>
              </div>
              <div style="background:#FBF6F6;border:1px solid #EFDDDD;border-left:3px solid #791F1F;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:9px">
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#5A2020;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Across seven sessions, nothing in his account is a client relationship, a decision he made alone, or a request he turned down. The plan he was given does not contain any of those either.</p>
                <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#791F1F;background:#F7E3E3;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">what the record does not hold</span>
              </div>
              <p style="font-size:13px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 22px">An empty list here means the record does not cover it. It does not mean he cannot do it, and it is not evidence that he would not.</p>

              <!-- FIT FOR THE ROLE, AS THE PRODUCT ACTUALLY DOES IT.
                   A probation turns on this and it is the easiest thing in the
                   world to get wrong, so the product's own contribution read is
                   built to refuse the obvious version: position is null, always.
                   It never says meeting it or below it. What it produces is the
                   remit as written, what the record supports against it, what the
                   record does not reach, and the reason - and where a remit was
                   never defined it shows no read at all, because "an undefined
                   role is often the real problem, not the person".

                   That refusal is the feature. A read against a written
                   expectation is useful; the same read with a verdict stapled to
                   it is a performance rating, and the moment this product
                   produces one of those nobody tells it the truth again. -->
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px">Against what the role was defined as</div>
              <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:12px">Read against the remit as written. No verdict, because a remit is not a scale.</div>

              <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #D8D5CF;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;margin-bottom:9px">
                <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6B6560;margin-bottom:4px">The remit, as written at the start</div>
                <p style="font-size:14px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 12px">"Delivery lead. Owns client delivery end to end, including the relationship."</p>

                <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#085041;margin-bottom:4px">What the record supports</div>
                <p style="font-size:14px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 12px">Delivery, without exception, for seven weeks. Nothing late, nothing escalated by anybody else, and every claim of it checkable against something.</p>

                <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#8A5C1A;margin-bottom:4px">What the record does not reach</div>
                <p style="font-size:14px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 12px">The relationship half of the remit. No client contact, no decision made alone, no request declined. His plan did not ask for any of it, and nobody has raised it with him.</p>

                <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:4px">Why it reads that way</div>
                <p style="font-size:14px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Half the remit has been tested and half has not been reachable. That is a gap in what he has been given, not a read on what he can do - and until it is given, this ground cannot tell you either way.</p>
              </div>
              <p style="font-size:13px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 22px">No position, no rating, no percentage. Where a remit was never written down this section shows nothing at all rather than guessing, because an undefined role is usually the real problem.</p>

              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:6px">What we each mean by doing well</div>
              <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #0C447C;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:7px">
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Judgement. Whether a messy problem can be handed over and stay handed over.</p>
                <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#0C447C;background:#E7EFF8;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Claire</span>
              </div>
              <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #9B9590;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:7px">
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Clearing the queue each week and not being the reason anything is late. It is the only number anyone has named.</p>
                <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#6B6560;background:#EDEBE7;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Tobi</span>
              </div>
              <div style="background:#FAF9F7;border:1px solid #E8E6E3;border-left:3px solid #8A5C1A;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;margin-bottom:14px">
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px">Assumed client work came later, once he had proved he could deliver. Nobody has said otherwise in seven weeks.</p>
                <span style="display:inline-block;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:#8A5C1A;background:#FBF1E0;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px">Sophie, who works alongside him</span>
              </div>

              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:4px">What moved, session by session</div>
              <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:#9B9590;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:10px">His own account of his own work.</div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Weeks 1&ndash;3</div><div class="did">Queue cleared each week. 22 tickets closed in the first three, nothing past its date.</div><span class="ev">3 documents</span></div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Weeks 4&ndash;5</div><div class="did">Shadowed the Brightwater account. Drafted the client summary and passed it to Claire rather than sending it, not being sure it was his to send.</div><span class="ev">2 documents</span></div>
              <div class="l-del"><div class="who">Weeks 6&ndash;7</div><div class="did">31 tickets in week six, the highest yet. Sales asked for a same-day turnaround that was not realistic and he said he would check with Claire.</div><span class="ev">3 documents</span></div>

              <div style="background:#EEF4FB;border:1px solid #C9DDF3;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;margin-top:18px">
                <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:7px">The one thing to settle first</div>
                <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#12314F;line-height:1.65;margin:0">Which of the two is he being measured on for the rest of the ninety days, and what changes in his week if the answer is judgement rather than the queue?</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div style="border-top:1px solid #EDEBE7;background:#FAF9F7;padding:14px 22px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">
              <span style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.5;flex:1;min-width:220px">Everyone agrees on: the start date, the onboarding plan as written, and that nothing has been late.</span>
              <span style="font-size:11.5px;font-weight:700;color:#8A5C1A;background:#FBF1E0;border:1px solid #F0DFC0;border-radius:20px;padding:5px 12px;white-space:nowrap">Mixed &middot; 3 settled, 1 open</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- THE PRIVATE HALF, OUTSIDE THE PANEL ON PURPOSE. Shaped like the
               real postReportGuide: something you could say, something to ask,
               something someone else raised worth taking seriously - and like the
               real one, naming and quoting nobody. -->
          <div style="max-width:640px;margin:18px 0 0;background:#FAF9F7;border:1px dashed #C9C5BE;border-radius:10px;padding:16px 18px">
            <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9B9590;margin-bottom:10px">What only Tobi sees</div>
            <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 12px">Everyone in the Ground gets one of these each time a report lands, drawn only from their own record. Nobody reads anybody else's, and neither does the person running it.</p>
            <div style="margin-bottom:11px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something you could say</div>
              <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">"I have been working to the queue because it is the only target I was given, and I am not sure it is the one that matters to you."</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:11px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something to ask</div>
              <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">What would you want me deciding on my own by day ninety, and what is the first thing you would hand over to test it?</p>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:12px">
              <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0C447C;margin-bottom:3px">Something to take seriously</div>
              <p style="font-size:14.5px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;margin:0">The other view is that the queue is not what is being weighed. You have twice checked before saying no to something; asking for one of those to be yours to decide would be worth more than another good week on tickets. You were brought in to lead delivery, so proposing the next step yourself counts for more here than waiting to be handed it - and nobody is going to tell you that in week eight.</p>
            </div>
            <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.6;margin:0">Tobi's own note, in his own copy. His manager does not see it.</p>
          </div>

          <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:17px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.6;font-weight:600;margin:24px 0 0">Nothing he did was wrong against the target he was given, and at this level the target running out was his to raise too. Both halves are in the report. A month-three review shows neither, because by then it is a performance problem with a person attached.</p>
          <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-top:16px">Two people meant different things by doing well for seven weeks, and neither of them was hiding it. It was in both accounts from week one, on separate pages.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <script is:inline>
        (function () {
          var row = document.getElementById('l-cases');
          if (!row) return;
          var tabs = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.l-case-tab'));
          var cases = Array.prototype.slice.call(row.children);
          tabs.forEach(function (t, i) {
            t.addEventListener('click', function () {
              row.scrollTo({ left: cases[i].offsetLeft - row.offsetLeft, behavior: 'smooth' });
            });
          });
          /**
           * THE ROW'S HEIGHT HAS TO FOLLOW THE CASE YOU ARE LOOKING AT.
           *
           * A flex row is as tall as its tallest child whatever align-items says,
           * and these four are 3213, 2030, 1819 and 2588 tall. So on any case but
           * the longest there was up to fourteen hundred pixels of empty white
           * underneath it - the "giant white space at the bottom of the reports".
           *
           * align-items:flex-start stopped the SHORT cases being stretched, which
           * was a separate bug, and could never have fixed this one: the
           * container is still sized by the tallest child either way.
           *
           * So the row is given the active case's height explicitly, and it is
           * transitioned so switching does not jump. Re-measured on resize,
           * because these blocks reflow hard between one and two columns.
           */
          function fitHeight() {
            var nearest = 0, best = Infinity;
            cases.forEach(function (c, i) {
              var d = Math.abs(c.offsetLeft - row.offsetLeft - row.scrollLeft);
              if (d < best) { best = d; nearest = i; }
            });
            tabs.forEach(function (t, i) { t.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(i === nearest)); });
            row.style.height = cases[nearest].getBoundingClientRect().height + 'px';
            return nearest;
          }

          row.style.transition = 'height .25s ease';
          // Images and fonts settle after first paint, so measure once more.
          fitHeight();
          window.addEventListener('load', fitHeight);
          window.addEventListener('resize', fitHeight);

          // The tabs follow the scroll rather than driving it, so dragging,
          // swiping and clicking all end up saying the same thing.
          row.addEventListener('scroll', fitHeight, { passive: true });
        })();
      </script>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- WORK IS CARRIED OUT BY PEOPLE -->


  <!-- B10: mutual course-correction. Architecturally true, and checked before
       writing: the board's read guard admits ANY party to the ground, not only
       the initiator, so every person sees the same picture. The contribution
       card's `position` field is typed `null` - it cannot hold a score. Neither
       the board nor the report ever reads a conversation turn, so raw answers
       cannot reach a shared surface. The synthesis prompts carry an explicit
       "never quote or name anyone". No model in the schema records presence,
       last-active or any measure of time spent. -->
  <!-- White, so it does not run into the cream section on either side of it.
       Everything on this page alternates; this one had no background at all and
       inherited the same value as the block above. -->
  <div class="l-sec l-fade" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-bottom:1px solid #E2E0DB">
    <div class="l-wrap-sm">
      <p class="l-sec-label">Who it is for</p>
      <!-- "THE SAME PICTURE GOES TO EVERYONE IN IT" IS TOO ABSOLUTE, and it is
           wrong in exactly the case where it matters most.

           Fourteen field officers in fourteen districts who have never met
           should not be reading each other's accounts, and the product agrees:
           namesVisibleTo is `viewerIsLead || isSelf || isLead`, so a cohort
           participant sees themselves and the person running it, and everyone
           else stays a role. The page was overclaiming past behaviour that is
           already correct, which is the worst way to be wrong - it invites
           somebody to discover a limit that was actually a considered decision.

           So the claim says what the rule IS. Everybody gets a report at the
           same moment, built from the same accounts; how much of it is named
           depends on where you sit. -->
      <h2 class="l-sec-h2" style="margin-bottom:18px">Everyone gets the report, at the same moment.</h2>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px">Groundwork is not a way of reporting your team to you. Everyone in a Ground gets the report the moment it is released, built from the same accounts, so the person running it can correct course and the people doing the work can correct course themselves, before a small thing turns into an expensive one.</p>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px">Who is named in it depends on where you sit. The person running it sees everyone. Everybody else sees themselves and the person running it, and the rest stay roles rather than names. On a group who all do the same job in different places, that is the whole point: they are not there to read each other.</p>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:14px;color:#6B6560;line-height:1.75;margin-bottom:26px">The person running it also sees occasional notes on what is worth asking about. Prompts for a better conversation, never a verdict, and never anyone's raw answer.</p>

      <!-- Side by side, because the two lists are a CONTRAST and stacked
           vertically the reader never sees them together. -->
      <div class="l-facing">
      <div style="background:#F5F3EF;border:1px solid #E2E0DB;border-radius:10px;padding:20px 22px">
        <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#1A1916;margin-bottom:10px">What your team gets</div>
        <ul style="font-size:14px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.9;margin:0;padding-left:20px">
          <li>a place to say what is actually going on, without saying it to someone's face</li>
          <li>the quiet contributor's work on the record, not just the confident account of it</li>
          <li>a blocker heard while it still matters, instead of after the deadline</li>
          <li>the same picture the person running it has</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div style="border:1px solid #E2E0DB;border-radius:10px;padding:20px 22px">
        <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#1A1916;margin-bottom:10px">What Groundwork is not</div>
        <ul style="font-size:14px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.9;margin:0;padding-left:20px">
          <li>It does not monitor activity, screen time, or who is online. It holds no record of any of them.</li>
          <li>It does not score, rank, or rate anyone. There is no number attached to a person anywhere in it.</li>
          <li>It never shows anyone's raw answers, to you or to anyone else.</li>
          <li>It never says who said what about whom.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- WHY LEADERS MISS PROBLEMS SITS HERE NOW, JUST BEFORE "COMING BACK".
       It used to open the page, so a first-time visitor met the problem before they
       had any idea what the product was. The page now says what Groundwork does,
       shows four real reports, says who it is for - and only then explains the
       mechanism that loses the information, immediately before the section about
       walking back into it after two weeks away. The two belong together: one is
       why the picture goes missing, the other is what it is like to have it. -->
  <div class="l-sec l-fade" style="background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #E2E0DB">
    <div class="l-wrap-sm">
      <!-- B2: concrete about the mechanism of not knowing, rather than a list
           of personality types. The reader should recognise the process that
           loses the information, not be told people are different. -->
      <p class="l-sec-label">Why leaders miss problems</p>
      <!-- IT PUT THE WORD "HIDING" IN THE READER'S HEAD IN ORDER TO DENY IT.
           The old heading read "Nobody is hiding anything. The story just changes
           on the way to you." It answered an accusation the reader had not made,
           on a page whose whole argument is that this is not a surveillance tool,
           and the second half was passive: nothing changed the story, it just
           changed.

           The section is about a mechanism - information degrades as it passes
           through people - so the heading now states the mechanism. The idea is
           NOT relocated elsewhere; it is gone. -->
      <h2 class="l-sec-h2" style="margin-bottom:18px">Every update you get has been through someone else first.</h2>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px">As the company grows you stop seeing the work yourself. You rely on meetings, written updates, chat, and the managers between you and the people doing it. Each of those is someone's summary, and every summary leaves something out.</p>
      <!-- 6c: same words, more air. Now the section's one object rather than
           three bold lines inside a column of grey ones - these are the part a
           reader repeats afterwards, and they were the least visible thing on
           screen. -->
      <div class="l-beats">
        <div class="l-beat">Some people dominate meetings.</div>
        <div class="l-beat">Some avoid conflict.</div>
        <div class="l-beat">Some do not feel safe challenging a decision.</div>
      </div>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px">So the account you get is shaped by who is confident, who was in the room, and what they think you want to hear. Someone explains it brilliantly and delivers poorly. Someone else quietly does excellent work nobody records. Most people are simply trying to do a good job.</p>
      <!-- Given weight rather than being the fourth grey paragraph in a row.
           The words are untouched; only the size and colour changed. -->
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:17px;color:#1A1916;line-height:1.65;font-weight:600;margin:22px 0 16px">The result is not a shortage of information. It is that everyone believes they are talking about the same thing, and often they are not. By the time it surfaces, expectations have already moved and the work has already drifted.</p>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin:0">Groundwork gives managers and teams a better way to work through these moments.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- B6: every item below was checked against a real ground's output before
       being listed. Divergences, the central question, who-owns-what,
       dependencies and decisions all exist and were populated in a live
       seven-person ground. Nothing here claims Groundwork chases anyone or acts
       on your behalf - it tells you what is there when you look. -->
  <div class="l-sec l-fade" style="background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #E2E0DB">
    <div class="l-wrap-sm">
      <p class="l-sec-label">Coming back</p>
      <h2 class="l-sec-h2" style="margin-bottom:18px">You leave for two weeks.</h2>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px">Instead of asking ten people for updates and assembling the picture yourself, it is already there:</p>
      <!-- The most concrete promise on the page, and it was set as a bulleted
           paragraph. Lighter than the report panel on purpose: that is the only
           document on this page and nothing else should compete with it. -->
      <div class="l-back">
        <div class="l-back-row"><span class="l-back-tick">&#10003;</span><span>where people have started to see it differently, and on what</span></div>
        <div class="l-back-row"><span class="l-back-tick">&#10003;</span><span>who owns what, and who is waiting on someone else</span></div>
        <div class="l-back-row"><span class="l-back-tick">&#10003;</span><span>the decisions that are sitting unmade</span></div>
        <div class="l-back-row"><span class="l-back-tick">&#10003;</span><span>the one question worth asking before anything else</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="l-prose" style="font-size:15px;color:#4A5568;line-height:1.8;margin:0">You still run the conversations. Groundwork just means you start them knowing what happened.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="l-cta-sec l-fade">
    <h2 class="l-cta-h2">A clear picture of<br>your most important work period.</h2>
    <p class="l-cta-p">Your first 10 Grounds are free. A check-in takes about ten minutes.</p>
    <a class="l-btn-solid" style="padding:15px 36px;font-size:15px" href={`${APP_URL}/start`}>Start your first Ground</a>
    <p style="font-size:12px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.2);margin-top:14px">First 10 Grounds free. No card required.</p>
